  "documentation": {
    "range": "<p>This species is found in almost all of the coastal areas of the eastern Atlantic Ocean, ranging from the Shetland Islands in the north and Nambia in the south. It is also found in all of the coastal areas of the Mediterranean Sea, throughout the North Sea and in the Baltic Sea (Jereb <em>et al.</em> 2010). It is known from depths up to 500 m.</p>",
    "population": "No catch statistics are available as this species is usually mixed with&#160;<em>L. forbesii.</em>",
    "habitats": "<p>This species is a neritic species that inhabits temperate coastal waters. This species is mainly found over coarse sand substrates. It is neither pelagic nor benthic; it lives on the sea floor during spawning and displays pelagic behaviour at other times. During the day, animals, especially young animals, display vertical migration in relation to food. Horizontal migrations also take place due to environmental factors; animals migrate into deeper waters during the winter. Offshore and onshore migrations also take place in relation to reproduction. Eggs are laid in strings on a structure in shallow water. Duration of egg development depends on environmental factors such as temperature and oxygenation. Paralarvae are planktonic for two to three months.<strong>&#160;</strong>Males are larger than females, but females are heavier than males. This species' diet consists mainly of fishes and crustaceans. It is preyed upon by whales and fishes, including common dolphin, pilot whale, bottlenose dolphin, swordfish, greater amberjack and many other fishes and marine mammals (Jereb <em>et al.</em> 2010).</p>",
    "threats": "Low quality landing information (due to squids not being identified to species) and because most catches are made as bycatch in finfish fisheries mean that data quality is generally poor and it is very difficult to assess the level of exploitation (Boyle and Pierce 1994). However, analyses indicate that juvenile&#160;<em>L. vulgaris</em>&#160;are subject to high fishing mortality when recruitment of the congener&#160;<em>L. forbesii</em>&#160;is low and exploitation levels have been shown to be above optimum in the English Channel (Jereb <em>et al.</em> 2015).",
    "measures": "There are no species-specific conservation actions in place for this species<em>.&#160;</em>There are no regular stock assessments and management is largely limited to landing-size regulations in southern Europe (Jereb <em>et al. </em>2015). Better assessments are required to ensure that harvest is sustainable.",
    "use_trade": "<p>This<em>&#160;</em>is one of the most economically important myopsid squid species due to its high market value. It is fished throughout its range, primarily as bycatch by bottom and pelagic trawl fisheries. This species is caught during the day using otter trawls and purse seines. These methods are used in international fisheries located off Portugal, on the West African Banks and in the western Mediterranean. No separate catch statistics are available as this species is usually mixed with <em>L. forbesii</em>&#160;(Jereb <em>et al.</em> 2010).</p> <p></p>",
    "rationale": "<em>Loligo vulgaris </em>has been assessed as Data Deficient because there are no statistics available to determine the level at which this species is currently being fished. Fishing occurs throughout this species' range and so pressure may be unsustainable, at least in parts of its range. Better stock and harvest monitoring are required to collate better data and ensure sustainable harvesting and improved reassessments in future.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": "No type locality was designated for this species (Sweeney 2012)."
  },
